course: dictionary
lesson: 3486
title: Famously: well known, or very well
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Famously means in a way that many people know about. When you meet it, ask: is this a well-known thing? «Newton famously watched an apple fall» tells you that the apple story is one that lots of people know.
explain: Famously can also be old-fashioned and informal, meaning very well. «The two puppies get on famously» means they get along very well and are great friends. The sentence is about puppies, so the clue is get on, which tells you about friendship.
explain: Try the swap check. Put well known, or very well, in place of famously. «Newton well-known watched an apple fall» does not sound right, but «The two puppies get on very well» does, so this is the old-fashioned meaning.
fact: Famously means in a way that many people know about.
fact: Old-fashioned, informal use: famously can mean very well, as in «get on famously».
fact: «Foley artists famously use halves of coconut shells for horses' hooves.» Many people who study film know this story.
fact: Famously is an adverb, so it tells you more about a verb, such as how something is known or how two people get on.
fact: A good check is to swap in well known or very well and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Newton famously watched an apple fall.» What does famously tell us? | It happened very slowly | It was only one person's guess | It was a secret that nobody knew | * It is a well-known story
exercise: Two puppies «get on famously». What does this mean? | They are famous dogs on television | They ran away from each other | * They are great friends and get along very well | They are both fast asleep
exercise: «Foley artists famously use halves of coconut shells for horses' hooves.» Which word means about the same as famously here? | * Well known | Quietly | Rarely | Secretly
test: In «Patrick Star is famously brainless», what does famously tell us? | Patrick is a real starfish with many legs | Patrick has a huge brain | * It is a well-known joke about Patrick | Patrick is hiding his brain
test: Swap famously for very well in «The two puppies get on famously». Does it still make sense? | * Yes, it means they get on very well | Yes, but only in a history book | No, because puppies cannot talk | No, it makes no sense at all
exam: Which sentence uses famously in its old-fashioned sense of very well? | Foley artists famously use coconut shells | Newton famously watched an apple fall | * Nadia and Sam get on famously at school | The BBC famously showed spaghetti growing on trees
